%% text 
%% text "This song is dedicated to my grandmother. She holds a tradition of putting 
%% text flowers on our family's graves for Memorial Day. Each year we'd fill coffee cans 
%% text full of irises, peonies, poppies and day lilies. I loved this whole process from 
%% text start to finish. I loved being in a cemetery, adorning the graves, feeling like we 
%% text are taking care of the spirits of those who aren't with us anymore. Every year 
%% text we'd wander the graveyard looking for one grave: her name was Viola. I thought 
%% text that was the coolest name ever. She wasn't related to us, but my grandmother 
%% text knew she was a lone woman and had been friends with her so it was a 
%% text tradition to put flowers on her grave too. But the thing was, we could never 
%% text find her grave, so we wandered the graveyard every year, with my grandmother 
%% text talking about each grave, folks she knew, often saying, "Oh right, it's just over 
%% text here now, " but every year it was the same, waltzing around this beautiful 
%% text graveyard with coffee cans full of flowers."  --Sarah Alden
