X:707
T:Song of the Books, The
T:Amhr\'an na Leabhar
T:Cuan Bh\'eil Inse
T:Valencia harbour
%%writefields W false    % remove or comment out to print the translation below the sheet music
%
N:
N: ...a famous 'traditional epic' written by Kerry poet Tom\'as Rua \'O S\'uilleabh\'ain (1785-1848).
N: 
N: Under British rule the Irish language went into decline as it was banned out of public life (schools, courts etc),
N: and children were taught through English. 
N: However, illegal secret schools known as hedge-schools were set up for Catholic children (as a response to the
N: prohibition of Catholic education in the country and the failure of the government - English and Protestant - 
N: to provide schools of any sort for the Catholic majority), where children were taught through the Irish language. 
N: 
N: \'O S\'uilleabh\'ain had been acting-schoolmaster in one of these hedge schools at Caherdaniel but was forced to
N: transfer to Portmagee (Co. Kerry) when another schoolmaster was appointed to the permanent position. 
N: As he was leaving, he placed his treasured books for transport on a boat going from Derrynane to Goleen
N: (G\'oil\'in, Valentia Harbour), while he himself travelled by road. 
N: The boat struck a rock and was lost, tragically along with the priceless collection of Irish books.
N: This lonely and desolate lament reflects the despair of those times. 
%
L:1/8
Q:1/4=100
%%printtempo false
S:Chris Partington <ChrisTheFiddlerPartington:Hotmail.com> tradtunes 2009-4-2
C:Tomás Rua Ó Súilleabháin,(1785-1848)
Z:transcribed by Paul de Grae,edited by Chris Partington
F:http://trillian.mit.edu/~jc/music/abc/air/Amhran_Na_Leabhar_3.abc	 2022-05-23 204628 UT
Z:abc-edited-by: AW (additional info and notes from YouTube)
F:https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=RQgXY07sz8Q
K:Edor
%%text Freely
B2 |\
(E3F) (G3A) | Be-e4-ef | e3 d B3 A | Bc d4 e2 |\
E3 F G3 F | GA B4 BA | G2 E4 ED | E4-Ez B2 |
E3 F G2 A2 | Be-e4-ef | e3 d B3 A | Bc d4-dz |\
E2-EF G3 F | GA B4 BA | G2 F E4 D | E4-Ez e2 ||
e2-ed e3 d | ef g4 f2 | e3 d B3 A | B4-Bz Bc |\
d2-dc d3 c | dd e4 ed | B3 A G3 A | B4-Bz B2 |
E2-EF G2 A2 | Be-e4 ef | e3 d B3 A | Bc d4-de |\
E2-EF G3 F | GA B4 BA | G2 F E4 D | E6 |]
%
W: To the harbour of Valentia Island I happened to go
W: by the beautiful inlet of Dairbhre
W: where the maritime goods are sailed
W: overseas, far away
W: 
W: In Portmagee I stopped a while
W: for the sake of education
W: to be amongst them for a while
W: as a schoolmaster
W: 
W: Soon the event was heard of
W: by all, alas
W: that the 'Eona\'in' was lost overboard
W: ...the mighty vessel
W: 
W: My heart throbbed with pain
W: because the boat of that brave captain
W: it was better for the country had it waited a while
W: to be under favourable auspices
W: 
W: 
W: If only I could walk through Ireland and Scotland
W: France, Spain and England
W: and yet again if only I could tell
W: every corner of the earth
W: 
W: I could not alas retrieve the multitude of books
W: knowledge and usefulness
W: more than anything had shaped my belongings
W: which went astray
W: 
W: Woe is me, my agony in that loss
W: I was abandoned
W: by that most cursed course
W: over which I now grieve
W: 
W: may the curse of God and the church
W: be on those vile, deadly rocks
W: for drowning the boat without a storm
W: without a gale, without a wind
W: 
W: 
W: There was a vast number of Irish books
W: which I didn't mention in my speeches
W: books of the blessed Leinstermen
W: the finest on this earth
W: 
W: The delightful, clever, fine husbandman
W: properly sowed his seed with relish
W: he bestowed heather and green furze on rugged hills
W: until the bright white hay
W: 
W: I pot a stop to my words
W: which afflicted me
W: and I shall send nothing by sea
W: ever again as long as I live
W: 
W: Praise be to the bright King of the Angels
W: I regained my health
W: and from the storm yon crew
W: without drowning safely returned
W: 
W: [Translation from the video posted to YouTube by M. M\'aire N\i Sh\'uilleabh\'ain]
